WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.020
How can deadlock be prevented?

2
00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:05.040
Prevention arranges the resource protocol

3
00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:07.100
so at least one necessary

4
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:10.540
deadlock condition cannot hold. Identify

5
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:11.920
the condition your rule breaks

6
00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:13.760
and the practical cost of

7
00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:14.200
that rule.

8
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.020
Mutual exclusion means some resource

9
00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:19.080
cannot be shared simultaneously by

10
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.640
all contenders. A read-only object

11
00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:23.340
may be shareable, but an

12
00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.640
exclusive device operation or mutable

13
00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:27.480
critical section can require an

14
00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.020
owner. Making every resource freely

15
00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:31.720
shareable is therefore not a

16
00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:32.740
universal remedy.

17
00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:35.080
Hold and wait means retaining

18
00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:37.120
an acquired resource while requesting

19
00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.420
another. Requesting the whole required

20
00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.600
set atomically before starting can

21
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.020
remove this condition. It may

22
00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:47.220
reduce utilization because a process

23
00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.280
reserves resources before it needs

24
00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:49.600
them.

25
00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.760
Alternatively, a process that cannot

26
00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:54.720
acquire its next resource can

27
00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:55.940
release what it holds and

28
00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:58.580
retry later. That strategy depends

29
00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:00.100
on being able to undo

30
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:01.880
or restart the work safely.

31
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.440
Retrying also requires a fairness

32
00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.060
argument if you want to

33
00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:07.140
rule out starvation.

34
00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:10.380
No preemption means resources cannot

35
00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:11.940
simply be taken back from

36
00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.220
their holders. Some resources can

37
00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.780
be saved and restored, while

38
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.620
others cannot be safely revoked

39
00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:19.760
halfway through an operation. A

40
00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:21.500
prevention answer must name the

41
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.300
resource model instead of assuming

42
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:24.800
arbitrary forced release.

43
00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.380
Circular wait is prevented by

44
00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:28.880
assigning a total order to

45
00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.120
resource types and requiring every

46
00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:32.420
process to acquire them in

47
00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.580
increasing order. For locks A

48
00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.660
before B, both processes must

49
00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.900
acquire A before requesting B.

50
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.860
A cycle would require the

51
00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:42.540
ordered resource ranks to increase

52
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:43.800
and return to their starting

53
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.380
point, which is impossible.

54
00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:48.140
Replay the two-lock example with

55
00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:50.620
that order. P zero acquires

56
00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:51.700
A and B while P

57
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:53.400
one waits for A without

58
00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:54.960
holding B. P zero can

59
00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:56.900
finish and release both, allowing

60
00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:58.220
P one to continue.

61
00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.620
Dining philosophers offers several different

62
00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:05.220
prevention rules. Limit simultaneous contenders,

63
00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.840
acquire a pair atomically, or

64
00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.760
impose a consistent stick order.

65
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:12.380
Explain the particular wait cycle

66
00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.500
each rule prevents rather than

67
00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.060
treating the names as magic.

68
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.380
Avoidance takes another approach: examine

69
00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:21.840
a proposed allocation and preserve

70
00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.960
a safe completion possibility under

71
00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.640
declared claims. That is different

72
00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.480
from permanently forbidding hold and

73
00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.540
wait. A prevention rule and

74
00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.020
an avoidance test can both

75
00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:33.900
be conservative for different reasons.

76
00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:35.900
Things to take note of.

77
00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.760
Name all four necessary conditions,

78
00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:40.700
then break at least one

79
00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.620
with a concrete resource protocol.

80
00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.200
Deadlock freedom alone does not

81
00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.920
guarantee fairness, good utilization, or

82
00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.160
the absence of starvation.
